    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Syst??????me de cote ??????nerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="10" build="20288">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.10b20288</English>
                <French>v15.10b20288</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16" build="20288">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16b20288</English>
                <French>v1.16b20288</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>The infiltration test has shown air leakage from the following house components:

-  Receptacles

-  Some windows

-  Rear door

-  Cumbustion air supply

-  Furnace chimney



During the infiltration test walkthrough, we notice air leakage on the items listed above. Airtightness is very important to achieve energy savings.

</airSeal>
                <ceilings>At the time of the audit, the attic insulation inspection was not possible. We estimated the insulation level according to the year of construction. However, we recommend the addition of insulation to meet the city of Whitehorse standard of R-60. We recommend the same thing for the cathedral ceiling, but in this case, we suggest adding as much as possible according to the space available.</ceilings>
                <VentilationSystem>The result of the infiltration test has shown that your house's natural air exchange is not enough. We recommend the installation of an HRV (Heat Recovery Ventilator) HVI (Home Ventilation Institute) and Energy Star certified, to ensure proper ventilation inside your home.</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Have you already thought about having a heat pump. This type of heating system can use up to 3 times less energy than a typical baseboard and needs electricity to operate. However this kind of system can?????????t operate on temperature below -25 degrees C, it then will require a secondary system to maintain the heat. In this case, the furnace can ensure the heat and the ducting system can be used by the heat pump to supply heat in the entire building. You might be eligible to participate in the Heat Pumps pilot project, please follow the link below for more information;

https://yukon.ca/en/news/expanded-rebates-renewable-home-heating-now-available



</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>The depressurization test performed as shown a high risk of combustion gas spillage. Therefore, we recommend verifying your carbon monoxide detectors regularly.</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
